<Layout title="Dismissal Deadline in Spain | López Márquez Abogados" description="General information about the time limit for challenging a dismissal in Spain and why the exact notification date matters.">
<PageHero eyebrow="Resource" title="How Long Do You Have to Challenge a Dismissal in Spain?" description="Spanish procedural law provides a general 20-working-day deadline for challenging a dismissal. The exact calculation and any procedural interruption must be checked against the circumstances." primaryLabel="Contact the firm" primaryHref="/en/contact" secondaryLabel="More resources" secondaryHref="/en/resources" note="General information · Check the current official source before acting" />
<section class="section"><div class="container prose"><h2>The date you were notified matters</h2><p>A dismissal challenge is subject to a short statutory period. Article 103 of the Social Jurisdiction Act sets a general period of 20 working days. The precise calculation depends on the dates and procedural circumstances of the case.</p><h2>Why you should not work from an estimate</h2><p>Start with the exact notification date and preserve the letter or evidence of communication. A mistake about the date can change the available time to act.</p><p><a href="https://www.boe.es/buscar/act.php?id=BOE-A-2011-15936#a103" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read Article 103 of the Social Jurisdiction Act at the BOE →</a></p><div class="callout"><strong>Important:</strong> general information cannot calculate your individual deadline. Where a dismissal has already been notified, a specific review should be made promptly.</div></div></section>
<CTA title="Need to check your specific situation?" text="Tell us your dates, status and what you are trying to achieve." label="Contact the firm" />
</Layout>
